implementationPlan.note:
(a) DoD-3, end to end through production surfaces only. Test "replaces a verified fence and retires its refs from the authentication fence":
1. a seeded harness store fences ITSELF via ensureGenesisRecoveryBinding -> INSTALLED;
2. createRestorePort(...).inspect() reports GENESIS_FENCED;
3. a session is opened through the production session.open command, which stamps the session with the refs it reads off the ACTIVE slot — the GENESIS refs, chosen by production code and never by the test — and createSessionAuthenticator returns AUTHENTICATED;
4. the EXISTING flow runs: anchoredIncarnation + port.resume(restoreRequest(...)) -> QUIESCED. No second installer exists;
5. inspect() is now INSTALLED with the restore record;
6. readCurrentRecoveryAuthenticationBinding names the RESTORE incarnation and explicitly NOT the genesis one — atomic replacement through the existing restore transaction;
7. the same credential now authenticates to verdict REFUSED with refusal {code: "SESSION_REPLAYED", httpStatus: 401, layer: "IDENTITY"} — the exact code AND the layer, not merely that authentication stopped working.

That last assertion is what makes "the old genesis refs no longer satisfy the fence" a measurement rather than a claim: the session is unchanged, only the slot moved, and the authenticator refuses on incarnation replay.

(b) DoD-1 reachability. restore-controller-reachability.test.ts gains "classifies a FRESH production store as genesis-fenced, not unreadable": a fresh createStoreDependencies — the same provider the daemon bin loads — inspects to GENESIS_FENCED, and explicitly NOT to RESTORE_RECORD_UNREADABLE and NOT to ABSENT. Nothing hand-installs a row; the provider's own genesis installer writes it, so this proves the INSTALLER and the classifier agree rather than proving a decoder can read bytes a fixture wrote. A second provider over the same path is asserted too: the anchor is re-offered, recognised as a repeat, and the classification is stable across boots.

DRILL PROVING THE REACHABILITY TEST IS THE REAL SYMPTOM, not a new test that always passed: I restored the pre-change line `if (record === null) return controllerRefusal("RESTORE_RECORD_UNREADABLE")` (printed the applied line, 62; chained with &&) and the new test failed with exactly the task's reported symptom —
  AssertionError: expected { authority: 'NONE', ...(6) } to match object { ok: true, outcome: 'GENESIS_FENCED' }
  -   "outcome": "GENESIS_FENCED"
  +   "outcome": "REFUSED"
and the other 3 reachability tests stayed green, so the drill was targeted rather than a broken environment. Restored with Edit, sha256sum -c back to pre-drill bytes: OK.

Note on the pre-existing reach-refuse test, which still asserts inspect() === {ok: true, outcome: "ABSENT"} and still passes: that store has an ANCHORED restore incarnation, so genesis DEFERS and the ACTIVE slot really is empty. It is correctly unaffected, and its continued green is evidence the DEFERRED path did not regress.

Verification: tsc exit 0 (one TS2339 fixed — AuthenticationResult needed narrowing to the REFUSED arm before reading .refusal). vitest src/recovery/ src/identity/ -> 23 files, 455 passed (455), exit 0. restore-controller.test.ts 48, reachability 4.

Cardinality test "sweeps an exact, hand-counted set of cases" added ahead of the it.each, so a table that silently produced ZERO cases fails instead of passing while testing nothing. Every number and name in it is a hand-written literal:

  expect(FENCE_CASES).toHaveLength(12);
  expect(GENESIS_CONJUNCTS).toHaveLength(7);
  expect([...new Set(FENCE_CASES.map(c => c.conjunct))].sort()).toEqual(
    ["ANCHOR","DECODES","DERIVATION","ORIGIN","PROJECT_CONTEXT","PROOF","ROW_REFS"]);
  expect(new Set(FENCE_CASES.map(c => c.name)).size).toBe(12);

Each conjunct is NAMED, so dropping a case fails this assertion rather than quietly shrinking coverage to whatever is left. The distinct-name assertion stops two entries collapsing into one report.

THE ASSERTION THAT DOES THE REAL WORK is against the PRODUCTION surface, not a copy of it:

  expect([...new Set(FENCE_CASES.map(c => c.reason))].sort())
    .toEqual([...GENESIS_FENCE_REJECTIONS].sort());
  expect(GENESIS_FENCE_REJECTIONS).toHaveLength(8);

A count computed from the generator cannot police the generator, so the table's reason set is required to equal the classifier's own exported rejection list. Adding a rejection code to production with no case here fails immediately.

DRILL PROVING THAT COUPLING IS LIVE: I appended a 9th reason "UNSWEPT_NEW_REASON" to GENESIS_FENCE_REJECTIONS in the production module (printed the applied line, 47; chained with &&). Exactly one test went red, on the right assertion:
  AssertionError: expected [ 'ANCHOR_ABSENT', ...(7) ] to deeply equal [ 'ANCHOR_ABSENT', ...(8) ]
  -   "UNSWEPT_NEW_REASON"
The other 48 stayed green, so the drill was targeted and not a broken environment. Restored with Edit, sha256sum -c back to pre-drill bytes: OK.

Coverage accounting, stated explicitly so a reviewer can count it: 12 failing cases spanning all 7 conjuncts and all 8 rejection reasons, PLUS the passing case, which is its own named test ("verifies the fence a production genesis install actually wrote") and asserts verified:true with origin GENESIS and both refs matching the row — 13 strict-classification cases in total. Each failing case asserts THREE things in the sweep body: the exact controller code and layer (expectRefusal at DAEMON_RESTORE_CONTROLLER with RESTORE_RECORD_UNREADABLE), that it is neither ok nor ABSENT, and the exact classifier conjunct.

